* 2. Regression Model
****************************************************
*Choose one dependent variable (DV), preferably continuous
	*Dependent Variable chosen: FY24Income (per capita personal income, in dollars, in 2024 for each U.S. state)

*Select one or two independent variables (IVs)
	*Independent Variables chosen: bachelorspercent (percentage of people with a bachelor's degree) & region 	

*Label your model and include a brief comment on your hypothesis
	*𝒀 = 𝜷𝟎 + 𝜷 ∗ 𝑿 + 𝜺
	*Y= FY24Income
	*X= bachelorspercent
	
	*Hypothesis: States with a higher percentage of residents holding bachelor's degrees will have 	higher per capita income levels.
	
reg FY24Income bachelorspercent

****************************************************
* 3. Visualize Relationships
****************************************************
*Create a scatterplot with a fitted regression line

*Use title() and label() for clarity
twoway (scatter FY24Income bachelorspercent, ///
        mcolor(dkorange) msymbol(smdiamond) ///
        xlabel(20(5)50) ylabel(50000(10000)100000) ///
        legend(off)) ///
       (lfit FY24Income bachelorspercent, lcolor(navy) lwidth(medium)), ///
       title("Per Capita Income & Bachelor's Degree Attainment") ///
       xtitle("Percent of Adults with Bachelor's Degree") ///
       ytitle("Per Capita Income (Dollars)")

graph export "Relationship Between Per Capita Income & Degree Attainment.png", replace
	   
*Comment on whether the plot aligns with your regression results
	*The scatterplot shows a positive relationship between adults with a bachelor's degree and per capita income, which aligns with the regression table output. States with higher educational attainment tend to have higher income, which aligns with the positive slope estimated in the regression model.

****************************************************
* 4. Visualize Predicted Values
****************************************************
*Use margins and marginsplot to display predictions

reg FY24Income bachelorspercent
*predict FY24Income at bachelorspercent=20, 25, 30, ..., 70
margins, at(bachelorspercent=(20(5)70))
marginsplot, ///
    title("Predicted Per Capita Income by Education Level") ///
    xtitle("Percent of Adults with Bachelor's Degree") ///
    ytitle("Predicted Per Capita Income ($)") ///
    plotopts(lcolor(red) lwidth(medium)) ///
    xlab(20(5)70)
	
graph export "Predicted Per Capita Income by Degree Attainment.png", replace
	   

*Interpret what the trends reveal in policy terms
	*The marginsplot shows a positive trend: as the percentage of adults with a bachelor's degree increases, predicted per capita income rises. This suggests that investing in higher education can boost state income levels, so policies that expand access to college, improve graduation rates, or support workforce upskilling are likely to generate higher economic outcomes. States with lower educational attainment could particularly benefit from such initiatives to reduce income gaps across regions.

